{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And He breathed into his nostrils the breath of life</b> — This verse hints to us the excellence of the soul, its foundation and its secret, for it mentioned concerning it the full Name, and said that He breathed into His nostrils the breath of life, to inform us that it did not come into him from the elements, as He hinted concerning the moving soul (Genesis 1:20), nor did it come through emanation from the separate intelligences, but it is the spirit of the great HaShem — from His mouth knowledge and understanding — for one who breathes into the nostrils of another gives from his own soul. And this is what is said (Job 32:8), \"ונשמת שדי תבינם\" (and the breath of Shaddai gives them understanding), for it is from the foundation of understanding by way of truth and faith. And this is their statement in Sifrei (beginning of parashat Matot): vows are like one who swears by the life of the king, while oaths are like one who swears by the king himself; although there is no proof for the matter, it is a remembrance to the matter — \"חי ה' וחי נפשך\" (As HaShem lives and as your soul lives), etc. (II Kings 4:30). And in the midrash of Rabbi Nechunya ben HaKana (Sefer HaBahir, sign 57): What is \"וינפש\"? It teaches that the Sabbath day sustains all the souls, as it is said, \"וינפש\" (and He rested). From here you will understand the matter of the oath of Elohim, and the enlightened will understand.\n\nKnow that those who are clever in investigation differed concerning man. Some of them say (Ibn Gabirol the prince; Ibn Ezra on Exodus 23:25) that in man there are three souls: a growth-soul like that of plants, or one may say in it the power of growth; and he also has a moving soul, which Scripture mentioned (above, Genesis 1:20–24) concerning the fish and the beast and every creeping thing upon the earth; and the third is the rational soul. And some of them say (Eight Chapters of the Rambam, ch. 1) that this soul which is in man — from the mouth of the Most High — within it are found all three of these powers, and it alone is unique. And this verse, according to its plain sense, hints likewise, for it says that HaShem formed man, dust from the earth, and he lay cast as an unformed body like a silent stone, and the Holy One, blessed be He, breathed into his nostrils the breath of life, and then man became a \"נפש חיה\" (living soul), that he would move by means of it like the animals and the fish concerning which He said (above, Genesis 1:20–24), \"ישרצו המים שרץ נפש חיה\" (let the waters swarm with swarms of living soul) and \"תוצא הארץ נפש חיה\" (let the earth bring forth a living soul). And this is the meaning of \"לנפש חיה\" (to a living soul), that is to say that man became a soul in which there is life, after he had been as silent as the silence of earth, for the למ\"ד comes in inversions, like \"והיו לדם ביבשת\" (Exodus 4:9), \"ויהי לנחש\" (Exodus 4:3), \"וישם את הים לחרבה\" (Exodus 14:21).\n\nBut Onkelos (Targum Onkelos on Genesis 2:7) said: והות באדם לרוח ממללא (and it was in man a speaking spirit). It appears that his opinion accords with those who say that they are different souls, and this rational soul that HaShem breathed into his nostrils was in him as a speaking soul. And so it appears to me from the opinion of our Sages, from what they said (Sanhedrin 65a): Rava created a man; he sent him before Rabbi Zeira. He [Rabbi Zeira] spoke to him but he did not respond. He said: You are from the companions — return to your dust. And in Vayikra Rabbah (32:2): Rabbi Avin said, at the time a person sleeps, the body says to the soul, and the soul says to the נפש, and the נפש says to the angel. And likewise, \"רוחו ונשמתו אליו יאסף\" (Job 34:14) — He gathers to Himself His spirit and His soul — indicates according to its plain sense that they are two. If so, when Scripture says, \"וייצר ה' אלהים את האדם\" (and YHWH Elohim formed man), it refers to a formation of movement that man was created with, that is to say, possessing movement, for the formation is the vitality and the sensation; that which is called \"man\" is not the boundary of the dust. And just as He said, \"ויצר ה' אלהים מן האדמה כל חית השדה ואת כל עוף השמים ויבא אל האדם\" (Genesis 2:19), so after He formed him with sensation, He breathed into his nostrils the breath of life from the mouth of the Most High, to add this soul upon the aforementioned formation — \"ויהי האדם\" (and the man became), all of him, \"לנפש חיה\" (a living soul), for by this soul he understands and speaks, and by it he performs every deed, and all the souls and their powers belong to it. And this למ\"ד is a למ\"ד of possession, like \"אדני המלך לך אני וכל אשר לי\" (I Kings 20:4), \"לקונה אותו לדרתיו\" (Leviticus 25:30), \"לך אני הושיעני\" (Psalms 119:94). Or one may say that he entirely became a living soul and was transformed into a different man, for all his formations now belonged to this soul.\n\nAnd by way of truth, the enlightened will understand."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "In the citation from Sefer HaBahir, Ramban writes וינפש (with a ו), the form found in Exodus 31:17, rather than וינח (with a ח) found in some editions of the Bahir; the translation preserves his spelling. The parenthetical attributions in the Hebrew (e.g., Ibn Gabirol, Eight Chapters of the Rambam) appear to be editorial glosses in standard editions of Ramban's commentary and are rendered as part of the text as received."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:24:13.734635237Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}